          #|      #|# Quantitative charts
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a chart when the shape of the data matters more than reading individual values. Use a Markdown table when the reader needs a few exact values.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|| Question | Chart |
          #|      #|| --- | --- |
          #|      #|| How did a measurement change over time? | Line chart. Put time on the horizontal axis and leave gaps for missing observations. |
          #|      #|| How do categories compare? | Bar chart. Start the value axis at zero so bar lengths are comparable. |
          #|      #|| How are values distributed? | Histogram. Use consistent bins when comparing distributions and label the count or density axis. |
          #|      #|| How are two measurements related? | Scatter plot. Use one point per observation; correlation alone does not establish cause. |
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Label axes with units and give enough context to interpret the data, such as the period and source. Use an available plotting tool such as Matplotlib for a static plot, save it, and reference it as a file artifact. If changing inputs or filters is part of the answer, use an interactive HTML artifact instead. For storage, references, and interactive answers, see `guide:artifacts`.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams/diago" => (
          #|      #|# Diago / D2: architecture and dependencies
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a `diago` or `d2` fence; both use the same bundled Diago renderer. Diago supports a subset of D2, so start with basic nodes, edges, and containers rather than relying on syntax from a different D2 renderer.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- `id: Label` defines a node; `a -> b: Label` is a directed connection and `a -- b` is undirected. For imports, state that arrows point from importer to dependency. For data flow, label arrows with the operation or data they carry.
          #|      #|- `direction: right` lays out left to right; `down` lays out top to bottom.
          #|      #|- Braces hold attributes or child nodes. Use containers for real boundaries, such as an owning service, and dotted IDs to connect across them.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Architecture across a boundary
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```diago
          #|      #|direction: right
          #|      #|desktop: Desktop {
          #|      #|  panel: File panel
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|cloud: Cloud {
          #|      #|  api: Cloud API
          #|      #|  storage: File store {
          #|      #|    shape: cylinder
          #|      #|  }
          #|      #|  api -> storage: Read original bytes
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|desktop.panel -> cloud.api: Request download
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams/katex" => (
          #|      #|# Drawing flowcharts with KaTeX block math
          #|      #|
          #|      #|KaTeX can draw a flowchart from ordinary boxes and arrows, with text, formulas, or both as labels. The same technique works for a few connected components. For specific diagram types and features beyond KaTeX, see `guide:diagrams`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|In SeekMoon, put `$$` on separate opening and closing lines, with no code fence around the block.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## A short workflow
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Make a node with `\boxed{...}` and put prose inside `\text{...}`. Connect the nodes with `\longrightarrow`, or use `\xrightarrow{...}` when an arrow needs a label.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|$$
          #|      #|\boxed{\text{Submit request}} \longrightarrow
          #|      #|\boxed{\text{Review request}} \longrightarrow
          #|      #|\boxed{\text{Notify requester}}
          #|      #|$$
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## A decision with formulas
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `\begin{array}{...}` to place nodes and arrows on a grid: `&` separates columns and `\\` ends a row. Leave cells empty to line up the incoming arrow with the decision. Label each outgoing arrow with its condition or outcome. Here the two branches compute $y=|x|$.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|$$
          #|      #|\begin{array}{ccccc}
          #|      #| & & \boxed{\text{Input }x} & & \\
          #|      #| & & \downarrow & & \\
          #|      #|\boxed{y=x} & \xleftarrow{\text{yes}} & \boxed{x\ge 0\,?} &
          #|      #|\xrightarrow{\text{no}} & \boxed{y=-x}
          #|      #|\end{array}
          #|      #|$$
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Keep the grid narrow enough to read in the transcript. KaTeX does not route edges or rearrange the chart as it grows, and does not run TikZ.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams/mermaid" => (
          #|      #|# Mermaid: flows and states
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a `mermaid` fence. Pick a `flowchart` when the reader needs to follow actions and decisions. Pick `stateDiagram-v2` when the reader needs to understand which states are possible and which events cause transitions. If the project already uses Mermaid and the point is message order, use `sequenceDiagram` instead of trying to express the order in a flowchart.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Workflow and branches
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `flowchart TD` for top-down or `flowchart LR` for left-to-right layout. Give nodes short IDs and readable labels. Label the outgoing edges of decisions with the condition or outcome.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mermaid
          #|      #|flowchart TD
          #|      #|  Request[Download requested] --> Cache{Cached bytes ready?}
          #|      #|  Cache -->|Yes| Save[Save file]
          #|      #|  Cache -->|No| Fetch[Fetch original bytes]
          #|      #|  Fetch --> Save
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## State transitions
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Label arrows with the event that changes state, not the name of the new state. Use `[*]` for the initial or terminal point.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mermaid
          #|      #|stateDiagram-v2
          #|      #|  [*] --> Pending
          #|      #|  Pending --> Downloading: start
          #|      #|  Downloading --> Pending: retry
          #|      #|  Downloading --> Complete: saved
          #|      #|  Complete --> [*]
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "diagrams/plantuml" => (
          #|      #|# PlantUML: interactions and type relationships
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a `plantuml` or `uml` fence, with `@startuml` and `@enduml` inside it. SeekMoon renders a subset of PlantUML; don't assume every extension or layout option in the standalone renderer is available.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Sequence diagram
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a sequence diagram when the order of calls or responses matters. Declare the participants, list messages from top to bottom, and label replies that affect what happens next.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```plantuml
          #|      #|@startuml
          #|      #|participant Desktop
          #|      #|participant Cloud
          #|      #|Desktop -> Cloud : Request download
          #|      #|Cloud --> Desktop : Original file bytes
          #|      #|@enduml
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Class diagram
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use a class diagram when the distinction between an interface, an implementation, and their relationships is the point. Include only members relevant to the question.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```plantuml
          #|      #|@startuml
          #|      #|interface FileStore {
          #|      #|  + read(id)
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|class DiskStore
          #|      #|FileStore <|.. DiskStore
          #|      #|@enduml
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "git-shipping" => (
          #|      #|# Git and GitHub
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Publishing
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Push, open a PR, or merge only when requested. A request to push is not a request to open a PR. Never force-push a branch you do not own.
          #|      #|- Run the relevant local checks before publishing. In a PR you open, describe what changed, what you verified, and what you could not verify.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Attribution and session links
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- For commits you author, put `Co-Authored-By: SeekMoon <seekmoon@moonbitlang.com>` in its own final paragraph. End PR descriptions you write with `Generated with SeekMoon`. Add each marker once, only to your own work.
          #|      #|- For each PR opened during the session, including by a script or workflow, attach it once if `seekmoon` MCP is available: `@mcp.call("seekmoon", "artifacts_attach", { "type": "pull_request", "url": pr_url })`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## CI
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- To check a PR's CI, call `mbtx` with `filename="@builtin/ci-watch.mbtx"` and `args=["<PR number or URL>"]`. Watch after opening or updating a PR; add `--once` to `args` for a status snapshot only. The workflow is read-only. A pass covers observed checks on that head, not missing checks or merge readiness.
          #|      #|- For a failure, use the reported `failure_logs_argv` or follow the failed check's URL. Read logs from that exact run, not the latest run on the branch.
          #|      #|- If asked to fix CI, fix the cause and check the new head. Rerun a suspected infrastructure failure at most once when authorized, and report persistent failures or checks you could not verify.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "mcp" => (
          #|      #|# MCP services
          #|      #|
          #|      #|MCP servers can connect external services: chat, issue trackers, calendars, documents, and more. Their tools are not in your function list, and `@openseek_tools.call` does not reach them; a script uses the SDK's `mcp` package. When a task may involve such a service, or before concluding that something cannot be done, search the catalog and print the page:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mbtx
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|import {
          #|      #|  "moonbitlang/openseek_tools@0.4.2/mcp" @mcp,
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|
          #|      #|fn main {
          #|      #|  // No arguments list every tool; `query` ranks by keywords; `server` lists
          #|      #|  // one server's tools after its instructions, or says why it is unavailable.
          #|      #|  let page = @mcp.search(query="list open issues")
          #|      #|  println(page.text)
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|After the search script returns, read the tool descriptions and schemas, select a tool for the task, and construct its arguments in the next script. Call it with `@mcp.call(server, tool, arguments)`. Check `is_error` first, then match `data : Json?` against the discovered output schema and print only the fields or summary needed. For example, if discovery showed a `tracker` server with `list_issues`, a `repo` argument, and an `issues` result array:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mbtx
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|import {
          #|      #|  "moonbitlang/openseek_tools@0.4.2/mcp" @mcp,
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|
          #|      #|fn main {
          #|      #|  let result = @mcp.call("tracker", "list_issues", { "repo": "alpha" })
          #|      #|  if result.is_error { fail(result.content) }
          #|      #|  guard result.data is Some({ "issues": Array(issues), .. }) else {
          #|      #|    fail("Expected structured issues. Tool text: \{result.content}")
          #|      #|  }
          #|      #|  println("Issues on this page: \{issues.length()}")
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`data` comes from the server's `structuredContent`; `content` renders its content blocks as text. A tool can return either or both. `data` is absent when the server supplies no structured result or it exceeds the reply budget. Use `content` for text results. For a read that needs structured data, narrow an oversized query or reduce its page size. Images are saved as files listed in `images`; pass a path to `@openseek_tools.call("image", { "path": path })` to view one. To apply a known operation across catalog entries, such as the same check on every server, iterate over `page.tools` in one script. Each record has `server`, `name`, `description`, `input_schema`, and `output_schema`. A page holds a limited number of tools; its `next_offset` continues the listing.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|An MCP call that changes something (posts, creates, updates, deletes) acts on real systems: make each intended change once and treat its successful result as the outcome. Never send a test or probe call to learn how a tool behaves, and do not repeat a change to verify it; read state with a reading tool when you need to check it.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "moonbit-development" => (
          #|      #|# MoonBit development
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run `moon --help` to discover commands and `moon <command> --help` for the installed version's arguments and options.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Compiler-guided work
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run `moon check` via `@builtin/check.mbtx`, fix the reported locations, then recheck. For an API rename that needs compatibility, add the new name, deprecate the old one, and let the compiler identify callers to migrate instead of replacing text blindly.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|OpenSeek may append bounded `moon check:` feedback to MoonBit `edit` and `write` results. Use it for immediate feedback; run an explicit check when the summary is incomplete or you need to verify the final state. Review changes to generated `.mbti` files for unintended public API changes.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Look up APIs from the relevant module with `@builtin/ide.mbtx` and `args=["doc", "<query>"]`. Query a symbol or imported package alias, not an English description. If a qualified lookup misses, try a broader symbol query or inspect the imported package before guessing signatures. For structural searches that text cannot express, see `guide:moongrep`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Project setup
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Keep module metadata and dependency versions in `moon.mod`, and package imports, aliases, and targets in `moon.pkg`. Create `moon.mod` before checking a new project so `moon` does not pick up an unrelated parent module. Use `moon add` for registry dependencies rather than guessing versions. Core packages such as `moonbitlang/core/async/fs` still need a package import but no registry dependency.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Validation
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Check the affected target, run focused package tests, and format the changed sources. Test local package code with `_test.mbt` files, using `_wbtest.mbt` only when private access is needed; a standalone `moon run -e` snippet is not a substitute for testing a local package.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|For CLI changes, exercise the affected input modes (file arguments or stdin), successful stdout, and the exit status and diagnostics for invalid input. Use `mooncram` transcripts under `tests/cram/` for lasting coverage of stable CLI contracts. Keep live or networked tests opt-in.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "moongrep" => (
          #|      #|# Structural search with `moongrep`
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`moonx` runs `moongrep`, a structural (AST-based) search and lint tool for MoonBit. Use it when a text grep cannot express the shape you need ("all `inspect` calls whose `content` is a literal", "every `match` over `Some`/`None`", "every call of one function nested inside another"). No `--` separator is needed: the arguments after the coordinate are moongrep's own.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|This example combines several pattern features in a single call: a nested application, two `id` captures, `$_` for the arguments nobody cares about, `--guard` to keep only the inner callees whose name matches a regex, and `--exclude` for the one directory the defaults miss here.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```text
          #|      #|moonx moonbit-community/moongrep scan . --exclude editor/codemirror/demo --pattern '$(outer:id)($_, $(inner:id)($_))' --guard '{$inner: "^error_result$"}' --json
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The same call from a snippet — each argument is its own array element, because `@shell.Cmd` starts the program directly and no shell is there to split a string:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```mbtx
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|import {
          #|      #|  "moonbitlang/core/async/shell",
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|---
          #|      #|
          #|      #|///|
          #|      #|fn main {
          #|      #|  // A whole-repo scan can match more than `.output()` is able to capture, so
          #|      #|  // stream the records instead. `each_line` receives stdout only: the child's
          #|      #|  // stderr, where the skip warnings go, passes through to this program's own
          #|      #|  // stderr.
          #|      #|  let mut findings = 0
          #|      #|  let code = @shell.Cmd("moonx", [
          #|      #|    "moonbit-community/moongrep", "scan", ".",
          #|      #|    "--exclude", "editor/codemirror/demo",
          #|      #|    "--pattern", "$(outer:id)($_, $(inner:id)($_))",
          #|      #|    "--guard", "{$inner: \"^error_result$\"}",
          #|      #|    "--json",
          #|      #|  ]).each_line(line => {
          #|      #|    findings += 1
          #|      #|    // Keep the printed excerpt bounded; count the rest.
          #|      #|    if findings <= 20 {
          #|      #|      println(line)
          #|      #|    }
          #|      #|  })
          #|      #|  println("exit=\{code} findings=\{findings}")
          #|      #|}
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Scan roots and exclusions
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`scan` (and `lint`, which prepends the embedded builtin rules) scans recursively from the scan root — a directory or a single `.mbt` file; the default root `.` means the whole repository, which is the typical use. Hidden entries — any child name beginning with `.` (case-independent) — plus `_build`, `node_modules`, and `target` are skipped by default; `--exclude <name-or-path>` skips more entries. These exclusions apply to child entries during traversal: an explicitly selected scan root is still inspected even when its final path component matches one of the rules.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Non-hidden tool/eval/worktree directories are NOT in the default exclusions: a whole-repo scan descends into them and duplicates findings across nested checkouts, or aborts on a dangling symlink inside one. Hidden ones (`.claude`, `.worktrees`, `.moonagent`, `.repos`) are already covered by the leading-dot rule; exclude the rest up front — in this checkout: `--exclude editor/codemirror/demo`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Output
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use `--json` (not `--output-json`, which is `moon check`'s spelling) for agent-friendly output: one JSON object per line on stdout, `{"type": "finding", ...}`, carrying `file` (relative to the scan root, often `./`-prefixed), `rule_id`, `description`, `range` (1-based `line`/`column`), `matched_source`, and `source_context`. Diagnostics such as skipped blocks go to stderr as `{"type": "warning", ...}` lines. A scan with zero findings writes nothing and still exits 0. When parsing records with `@json.parse`, integer fields such as `range.start.line` pattern-match as `Number(value)` (a `Double`; call `.to_int()` on it), not `Int`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|A whole-repo scan can match many nodes — `@shell.Cmd(...).output()` cannot capture unbounded output, so a large scan hits the shell's output-limit error — stream with `.each_line()` (one record per callback line) to count or aggregate findings. The two streams stay separate either way: `.output()` keeps them apart as `out.stdout()` and `out.stderr()` (sharing one capture budget), and `.each_line()` streams stdout alone, leaving the warnings to the snippet's own stderr. To read the warnings back inside the snippet, use `.output()` and inspect `out.stderr()`, or redirect them with `stderr=ToFile(...)`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`--rules <dir>` and `--rule <file>` load YAML rule files, `--disable <rule-id>` drops a loaded rule, and `--verbose` traces traversal on stderr.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Patterns
          #|      #|
          #|      #|A `--pattern` is a MoonBit *expression* containing `$(name:kind)` metavariables — `exp`, `id`, `const`, `arg`, `pat`, `type` — plus `$_` to match anything without capturing. Matching is on the untyped CST: whitespace and comments do not matter, but syntax shape does (`Some(1)` does not match `Some($(some:id))`; `Ok`/`Err` branches do not match a `Some`/`None` pattern). A metavariable used twice must capture equal structure. `--guard '{$name: "regex"}'` filters `id` and `const` captures (substring match unless anchored with `^...$`).
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Labeled arguments match by label — `inspect($_, content=$(c:const))` finds the `inspect` calls whose expectation is a literal. `const` covers ordinary literals only: a multi-line `#|` string is not one, so the snapshot tests written that way need `content=$_`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When a pattern surprises you, `moonx moonbit-community/moongrep dump --expr '...'` or `moonx moonbit-community/moongrep dump --impl 'fn f { ... }'` prints the CST of a snippet. `moonx moonbit-community/moongrep docs RuleSpec` and `moonx moonbit-community/moongrep docs CLISpec` print the full specs.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Lint rules
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The builtin `lint` rules are advisory style checks — `catch_all`, for instance, flags deliberate `catch { _ => () }` swallows. Treat rule counts as signal, not a bug list, and read `matched_source` before acting on a finding.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Exit codes
          #|      #|
          #|      #|0 for help, dump, findings, no-findings, and parse warnings (no-match is NOT grep's 1); 2 usage; 3 invalid dump input; 4 rule-source failure; 5 invalid rule content (including a `--pattern` that is not a valid MoonBit expression); 6 missing/unreadable scan input; 7 output failure.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|The scanner follows symlinks, so a broken symlink anywhere under the scan root aborts the whole scan with 6. Source blocks whose syntax the bundled parser does not know yet (e.g. nested record-spread `is` patterns) are skipped with a stderr warning while exit stays 0 — treat skipped blocks as a blind spot.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "presentation" => (
          #|      #|# Presenting files, interactive answers, and sources
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- When an answer cites a real local file, link it as [main.mbt](absolute-path): plain label, the target is the file's absolute path, optional line number inside the target, angle brackets around a target with spaces (`[My Report.md](<absolute path with spaces>:3>)`), no backticks in or around the link, no `file://` or other URI schemes, no line ranges.
          #|      #|- Use the workspace-file fence only when a file already exists in the active workspace and the user should open it as a separate deliverable, such as a generated report, spreadsheet, or image. It renders as a file card in the desktop transcript and exported viewer:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|  ```openseek-file
          #|      #|  reports/prompt_leak_red_team_matrix.xlsx
          #|      #|  ```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|  Put exactly one path relative to the active workspace root on the line inside the fence. Do not use an absolute path, `file://`, `~`, or a path containing `..`; do not put file contents or multiple paths in this fence. Do not use this syntax for a file that has not been created or whose workspace location is unknown. When the user needs the contents in the answer, provide them directly; when citing a particular source file or line, use the ordinary Markdown link form above. Session-generated files use paths such as `scratch/artifact/answer.html` in this same fence. Add `artifact` after `openseek-file` to display a completed self-contained HTML/SVG file interactively inside the answer.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Workspace File Previews in SeekMoon
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When the user asks to view or preview a workspace file, or you finish a standalone visual deliverable such as an HTML page, PDF, image, or Office document, discover `preview_open` on the `seekmoon` MCP server and call it before your final response, unless the user requests otherwise. Save generated files first and pass the path relative to the workspace root. Keep a file reference in your response.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Routine source-code edits do not require automatic preview. Inline interactive artifacts retain their existing inline rendering. If the tool is unavailable or fails, provide the file reference and briefly report the limitation. The result `opening` acknowledges acceptance, not successful rendering.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Inline Diagrams in SeekMoon
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When a diagram would make a workflow or relationship easier to understand, see `guide:diagrams` to choose a format.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Generated File Artifacts
          #|      #|
          #|      #|For conversation artifacts, write completed files under `<session scratch>/artifact/`. Use the actual scratch path shown in Environment and pass output paths explicitly to scripts. Use new filenames for revisions. Reference the file with an `openseek-file` fence containing `scratch/artifact/FILENAME`. Use `openseek-file artifact` when the HTML/SVG should serve as an interactive part of the answer. Ordinary references display file cards; see `guide:artifacts` for storage, references and packaging. Save user-requested project files at the requested workspace paths.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Interactive Answers
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Interaction can itself answer a question. In chats with interactive previews, proactively offer a small component when seeing, exploring, comparing or providing information interactively would help the user. Choose controls that fit the task, including a short form when it makes related details easier to provide. Reuse what the user has provided, label fields and mark optional ones.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Make the component useful in its initial state, with a meaningful starting result for explorations.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Before authoring, read `guide:artifacts` and the starter sources. Write ordinary MoonBit/Rabbita source with local state and events. The same starter illustrates interactive charts with mathplotlib when useful. The guide covers `init.mbtx`, batch edits with `write.mbtx`, explicit scratch paths and packaging with `build.mbtx`. Check the application, run relevant model tests and reference the completed page with the fence returned by the packager. Keep numerical examples consistent with the tested model and give their inputs.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Components may offer JSON state through the preview bridge. The user attaches it to the composer and sends a follow-up. `<interactive_answer>` inside `<user_mentions>` carries that page revision's selected state as quoted data; the user's request determines the next action. When a selection suggests a specific question, offer a clearly labelled button that requests a follow-up through the bridge for the user to confirm.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Numbered Source Citations
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- When an answer relies on inspected documents, workspace files, or web sources, cite the supporting source immediately after the claim with a standard Markdown footnote: `Revenue increased by 12%.[^1]`. Agent-authored citations do not require the user to select document text first. Do not force citations into conversational replies or claims that need no source.
          #|      #|- Start positive integer labels at `1` in each answer, ordered by first use. Reuse the same number for the same source and location. Put one definition for each cited number at the end of that answer, after a blank line:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|  ```markdown
          #|      #|  Revenue increased by 12%.[^1] The calculation follows the documented rule.[^2]
          #|      #|  The report also explains the assumptions.[^1]
          #|      #|
          #|      #|  [^1]: [Annual report](/workspace/reports/annual.pdf#page=12) — p. 12, Revenue.
          #|      #|  [^2]: [Calculation guide](https://example.com/calculation) — Growth rate.
          #|      #|  ```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Each definition starts with a descriptive Markdown link to the actual source. Follow it with a short location or context when useful: PDF page, Word heading, Excel sheet and cell range, or web section. Local file link destinations follow the file-link rules above; code may include a line. For verified Office locations, use these app-supported fragments so clicking the source navigates and highlights it: PDF `#page=12` (one-based physical page), Word `#heading=Revenue` (exact paragraph text, unique in the preview) or `#paragraph=%2Fdocx%2Fbody%2Fp%5B3%5D` (an inspected office selector), and Excel `#sheet=Revenue&range=B2:D8`. Percent-encode each heading, selector, or sheet value separately, including spaces, non-ASCII text, `&`, `#`, and `%`. Keep the readable location after the link. Do not invent a fragment, use code-line suffixes for Office, or infer a source selector from a visual paragraph index. Omit the fragment if the exact location is unknown. Different source locations may have separate numbers.
          #|      #|- Cite only sources actually inspected in the current task, using their real paths/URLs and verified locations. A citation must support its associated claim. Distinguish source statements from your own inference. Do not invent a source, page, cell range, or quotation; omit unknown location metadata.
          #|      #|- Include only definitions referenced in the answer. Do not wrap citations in code, use raw HTML, or add a second manually numbered bibliography. The desktop renders the inline numbers and a consolidated Sources section from these definitions. Ordinary links remain appropriate for navigation and deliverables that are not evidence citations.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/design" => (
          #|      #|# Presentation Design Guidelines
          #|      #|
          #|      #|**General principle**: Skip any step below if the model does not support it. Any specification from the user—template, brand guidelines, color scheme, fonts, reference styles, attachments, and so on—takes precedence over this guide and any design system document.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 1. Understanding the Requirements
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Clarify the requirements**: Determine the purpose, audience, page count, and style direction, as well as the input type (topic only / full document / page-by-page outline and script).
          #|      #|- **Determine the page count**: Follow the user's specification first; otherwise align with the page-by-page outline or script; if neither exists, infer it from the topic and the amount of content, and explain your reasoning.
          #|      #|- **Do not interrupt by default**: When requirements are unclear, proceed with reasonable defaults; ask questions only for hard blockers such as an attachment that cannot be opened or contradictory intent.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 2. Design System and Color Scheme
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Choose a scenario**: Select the primary scenario based on the audience and the reader's task: analytical decision-making / business proposal / management report / academic research / education and training / technical engineering / brand creativity. Add a secondary scenario when necessary; if nothing matches, use a general-purpose approach rather than forcing a fit. For tasks that reproduce an image/PDF in Typst, analyze the original document directly without choosing a scenario.
          #|      #|- **Unified design system**: Use a single set of color, font, whitespace, and layout rules throughout the presentation; do not introduce conflicting constraints midway.
          #|      #|- **Color scheme priority**: User specification > the organization's primary color > the design system's recommendation. When the user states their organization, search for its logo first and extract the primary color.
          #|      #|- **Color constraints**: Avoid stacking red, purple, yellow, and green at the same time; unless specified by the user or a skill, use no more than three colors throughout. Text and background must have clear contrast.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 3. Fonts and Symbols
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Language and punctuation**: Default to Simplified Chinese unless the user requests Traditional; use full-width punctuation for Chinese, and half-width for English words, numbers, code, URLs, paths, technical identifiers, and mathematical content.
          #|      #|- **Font choice**: Choose fonts by language and tone. Use sans-serif for general reports; serif for academic, cultural, and formal presentations; rounded fonts for children's and educational scenarios; and monospaced fonts for code, number alignment, and metrics.
          #|      #|- **Font hierarchy**: Clearly define the division of labor between display and body fonts, unify the size baselines for headings, body text, captions, and sources, and establish hierarchy through size and weight rather than color.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 4. Content Organization and Page Layout
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Page task and title**: Before designing, clarify what each page should make the reader understand, believe, decide, or do; make titles state a judgment and avoid empty labels such as "Work Report", "Overview", or "Background".
          #|      #|- **Pacing**: Decide based on the content whether to include a table of contents page and section pages, balancing quick scanning with close reading of key points.
          #|      #|- **Table of contents layout**: Avoid stacking small text or leaving large blank areas; spread section blocks evenly across the page; if a single column does not fit, switch to two columns, arranging numbers and names separately.
          #|      #|- **Layout and alignment**: Choose the layout based on the content; do not default to thirds, quarters, or a 2×2 matrix. When using a horizontally parallel layout at the same level, default to equal widths with uniform alignment; use different widths only when you need to express a primary/secondary relationship.
          #|      #|- **Expressing hierarchy**: Unless the user requests otherwise, do not use rectangles or rounded cards to build hierarchy or achieve alignment; use line segments, whitespace, and contrast in font size and weight instead.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 5. Data, Charts, and Sources
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Do not fabricate**: Do not fabricate data, citations, customer cases, experimental results, or sources; mark missing materials as placeholders, assumptions, or to be supplemented.
          #|      #|- **Source attribution**: Pages involving external facts and data must state the source, date or time range, and methodology.
          #|      #|- **Structured presentation**: Use native tables or chart components for data comparisons, metric lists, parameter descriptions, and dimension breakdowns; do not use Markdown tables.
          #|      #|- **Legend legibility**: The colors of each legend in pie and donut charts must be easy to distinguish; you may use the primary color or a gradient of opacity.
          #|      #|- **Chart styling**: Check whether the default style is clear and consistent with the design system; adjust inappropriate axes, grid lines, legend boxes, and borders, and remove rainbow color schemes, 3D, gradients, and shadow effects that interfere with reading.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## 6. Images and Assets
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Image usage**: Place images as the content requires and avoid overuse; cover images must be high quality. Do not use abstract shapes as decoration on the cover, closing, or section pages; when visual assets are needed, prefer high-quality images.
          #|      #|- **Attached images**: Keep them as-is and do not crop them; let the layout adapt to the image.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/typst-layout" => (
          #|      #|# Typst Layout Guidelines
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Compile with the MoonBit Typst CLI
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run the CLI directly with `moonx cli/typst`:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```sh
          #|      #|moonx cli/typst compile slide.typ slide.pdf
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Run this from the directory containing `slide.typ`. `moonx` resolves and runs the CLI; there is no need to search SDK caches or create a MoonBit project.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Paragraph Justification
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Enable paragraph justification unless the user explicitly declines it:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|#set par(justify: true)
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Page Numbering
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When page numbers are needed, use Typst's automatic page numbering instead of hardcoding them. For example, to display the current page and total page count in a "1 / 3" style at the bottom right:
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|#set page(numbering: "1 / 1", number-align: right + bottom)
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Finding Local Fonts
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When you need to set a specific font in a Typst document, you can list the fonts available on the device in a way like the following (using Simplified Chinese SC as an example):
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|@shell.Pipeline([@shell.Cmd("moonx", ["cli/typst", "fonts"]), @shell.Cmd("rg", ["SC"])]).output()
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/typst-syntax-cautions" => (
          #|      #|# Typst Syntax Cautions
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 0 - Fetch the official reference instead of guessing
          #|      #|
          #|      #|When unsure about any Typst detail (element syntax, function arguments, symbol names), do not guess: fetch the official documentation and treat it as the source of truth.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Markup / model elements: https://typst.app/docs/reference/model/
          #|      #|- Math: https://typst.app/docs/reference/math/
          #|      #|- Symbol table: https://typst.app/docs/reference/symbols/sym/
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 1 - Do not treat operator characters as literal text
          #|      #|
          #|      #|`/` is the fraction operator, so `1/2` produces a stacked fraction, equivalent to `frac(1, 2)`. To display a literal slash as a math symbol, use `\/`.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|$1/2$        // stacked fraction
          #|      #|$frac(1, 2)$ // same as above (stacked)
          #|      #|$1 \/ 2$     // a literal slash 1/2
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 2 - Do not invent directive or keyword syntax
          #|      #|
          #|      #|[`limits`](https://typst.app/docs/reference/math/attach/#functions-limits) is a function, not a modifier token. Call it on the base to place its bottom and top attachments below and above the base instead of beside it.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|$limits(A)_1^2 != A_1^2$
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 3 - Understand repeated alignment markers
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Each `&` marks one alignment point. Multiple alignment points create alternating right- and left-aligned columns. `&&` is valid: it represents two consecutive alignment points and has the same alignment behavior as `& &`, as documented in the [official alignment reference](https://typst.app/docs/reference/math/#alignment).
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Example: the first `&` aligns the equations at `=`, and the following `&&` makes the annotation column left-aligned.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|$
          #|      #|  a &= b && "first equation" \
          #|      #|  c &= d && "second equation"
          #|      #|$
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Caution 4 - Do not use TeX macros for symbols or functions
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Typst symbol and function names never begin with a backslash (`\`). Do not write TeX macros such as `\dots` or `\cdots`. Use `dots` for a baseline ellipsis (`…`) and `dots.c` for a centered ellipsis (`⋯`).
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|$dots$   // … (baseline ellipsis)
          #|      #|$dots.c$ // ⋯ (centered ellipsis)
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|For other symbols you are unsure about, validate a code snippet with a command such as `moonx cli/typst eval '$dots$'`, or consult the [official symbol table](https://typst.app/docs/reference/symbols/sym/).
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/typst-touying" => (
          #|      #|# Touying for PDF Animations
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Use Touying only when the user needs PDF frame animations, such as progressive reveals with `#pause`, parallel reveal sequences with `#meanwhile`, or stepwise math equation animations. Use plain Typst for static slides.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|```typst
          #|      #|#import "@preview/touying:0.7.4": *
          #|      #|```
          #|      #|
          #|      #|PDF animations work by advancing through multiple pages (subslides) representing successive states of a logical slide. See the official [Dynamic Slides guide](https://touying-typ.github.io/docs/category/dynamic-slides).
          #|      #|
          #|      #|Consult the [official tutorials](https://touying-typ.github.io/docs/category/tutorials) when implementing Touying features. Follow their setup and examples, check compatibility with the pinned version, and verify unfamiliar APIs rather than guessing syntax.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- [Simple animations](https://touying-typ.github.io/docs/tutorials/dynamic/simple): `#pause` and `#meanwhile`.
          #|      #|- [Math equation animations](https://touying-typ.github.io/docs/tutorials/dynamic/equation): reveal equations and derivations step by step.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    "slides/writing" => (
          #|      #|# Writing Style for Presentations
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## General Principles
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Lead with the point**: Open with the main conclusion or question. Make titles convey specific information rather than generic section labels.
          #|      #|- **Be specific**: Name the subject, action, and result. Replace vague language such as "empower," "leverage," and "synergy" with facts and details. Avoid hype.
          #|      #|- **Write concisely**: Keep each sentence focused on one idea. Remove repetition, empty modifiers, and unnecessary setup while preserving conditions and explanations needed for understanding.
          #|      #|- **Make the reasoning clear**: Support claims with evidence and explain causes, tradeoffs, and scope. Distinguish facts, goals, and assumptions; do not present speculation as an established conclusion.
          #|      #|- **Use consistent terminology**: Explain concepts and abbreviations according to the audience's knowledge. Use the same name for the same concept throughout.
          #|      #|- **Match detail to purpose**: Make text for spoken presentations easy to scan; preserve complete arguments in materials intended for reading. Avoid rigid word limits.
          #|      #|- **Make recommendations actionable**: State the action, expected outcome, and necessary conditions. For collaborative work, specify owners and deadlines. Avoid closing with slogans.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Management Reports
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Use professional language appropriate to the presenter's role. Titles should state performance, causes, or requests so readers can follow the report by scanning its titles alone.
          #|      #|- Example title: "Q3 gross margin fell 3.2 percentage points, mainly due to higher raw material costs," rather than "Business Performance Report."
          #|      #|- Present results alongside their comparison baselines. Do not exaggerate achievements or omit unfavorable facts.
          #|      #|- Give actions a priority, owner, and deadline. Explain the expected impact and alternatives when requesting resources.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Technical and Engineering Presentations
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- State conclusions directly. Avoid suspense or saving the answer for a final reveal.
          #|      #|- Follow the decision sequence: goals and constraints → current state and bottlenecks → mechanisms and evidence → tradeoffs → rollout and rollback → risks and next steps.
          #|      #|- Use text to explain motivations, tradeoffs, and evidence; use diagrams to show call relationships and data flow. Avoid repeating the same information in both.
          #|      #|- Ground broad descriptions such as "high availability," "high concurrency," and "cloud-native" in specific SLAs, QPS figures, protocols, or components. Avoid marketing language such as "revolutionary," "redefining," and "disruptive."
          #|      #|- For performance claims, state the environment, versions, sample size, and load conditions. Use appropriate percentiles or confidence intervals rather than reporting only averages. Distinguish the current state, targets, assumptions, and verified conclusions.
          #|      #|- Close with a path to adoption or implementation, rather than only "Thank you" or an unsupported slogan.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Analysis and Decision Support
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Work from conclusions toward evidence: state the judgment in the title, then develop supporting claims that each stand on their own and have verifiable evidence.
          #|      #|- Explain chart findings, the claims they support, their implications for decisions, and the data definitions and limitations.
          #|      #|- Preserve depth in materials intended for reading. Make conclusions easy to scan and evidence available for close reading; do not impose the minimal text requirements of a launch presentation.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Business Proposals and Launches
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- **Persuasive proposals**: Develop the argument through pain points → solution and advantages → value. Support each advantage with data, a case study, or a comparison. Close with the action you want the audience to take.
          #|      #|- **Launch presentations**: Present one idea per slide, aiming for a single line of body text. Favor images and short sentences. A possible narrative is interest → conflict → solution reveal → significance.
          #|      #|- Choose the approach for the audience and purpose. Avoid applying launch-style suspense and minimal copy to proposals that require substantial evidence.
          #|      #|
          #|      #|## Education and Training
          #|      #|
          #|      #|- Use titles to pose learning questions or state conclusions. Give learners definitions or conclusions they can restate. When prompting thought, pose the question before developing the answer.
          #|      #|- Express learning objectives as observable actions, such as "explain the cause," "compare approaches," or "complete the procedure," rather than simply "understand," "master," or "be familiar with."
          #|      #|- Define new concepts before explaining their boundaries, components, or counterexamples. Do not introduce terms only in figure captions.
          #|      #|- Show the reasoning process, key tradeoffs, and common mistakes, not just the final answer.
          #|      #|- Motivate learning with real situations and connect it to practice through tasks, checklists, or exercises. Section summaries should answer the section's questions rather than repeat every slide title.
          #|      #|
          #|    )
          #|    _ => fail("agent-guide: unknown guide ID: " + id)
          #|  }
          #|}
          #|
          #|fn main {
          #|  let response : Json = match @env.args() {
          #|    [_] => {"version":1,"guides":[{"id":"artifacts","description":"Creating, packaging, and presenting session artifacts, including interactive answers."},{"id":"diagrams","description":"Choosing between prose, tables, charts, and diagrams, and selecting a diagram format."},{"id":"diagrams/charts","description":"Quantitative charts for trends, comparisons, distributions, and correlations."},{"id":"diagrams/diago","description":"Architecture and dependency diagrams with Diago or D2."},{"id":"diagrams/katex","description":"Explanatory diagrams drawn with KaTeX boxes and arrows."},{"id":"diagrams/mermaid","description":"Flowcharts and state diagrams with Mermaid."},{"id":"diagrams/plantuml","description":"Sequence and class diagrams with PlantUML."},{"id":"git-shipping","description":"Committing, pushing, opening pull requests, and investigating GitHub CI."},{"id":"mcp","description":"Discovering and using external services through MCP."},{"id":"moonbit-development","description":"Compiler-guided refactoring, project setup, and validation for MoonBit projects."},{"id":"moongrep","description":"Structural code search and linting with moongrep."},{"id":"presentation","description":"Presenting local files, previewing deliverables, citing sources, and offering interactive answers."},{"id":"slides/design","description":"Visual design for presentation slides."},{"id":"slides/typst-layout","description":"Slide layout in Typst."},{"id":"slides/typst-syntax-cautions","description":"Common Typst syntax mistakes in slide source."},{"id":"slides/typst-touying","description":"PDF slide animations and progressive reveals with Touying."},{"id":"slides/writing","description":"Writing and editing slide text."}]}
          #|    [_, id] => { "version": 1, "id": id, "format": "markdown", "content": content(id) }
          #|    _ => fail("agent-guide: expected zero or one guide ID")
          #|  }
          #|  println(response.stringify())
          #|}
          #|
        ),
      )
